/* Dev + check config: consume the SDK from source for a live edit loop, the way
   the Vite demos do. The `paths` below redirect @hilos/angular and @hilos/core
   to their `src`, so `ng serve` (the development build) and `npm run check` pick
   up an SDK edit immediately, with no ng-packagr rebuild. The production build
   keeps tsconfig.app.json, where the SDK resolves to its ng-packagr dist (the
   package `main`/`types`), so e2e and prod test the shipped FESM.

   Why `paths` and not the package `development` export condition (the mechanism
   the Vite demos use): @hilos/angular is built by ng-packagr, which owns the
   `exports` of its dist package.json and warns when the source package.json
   declares conflicting `types`/`default` conditions — so the source package
   stays plain (`main`/`module`/`types`) and the dev-source redirect lives here.
   The @angular/* pins collapse the duplicate copies the SDK reaches through its
   real path (NG0203/NG3004); they are restated here because a child tsconfig
   replaces `paths` wholesale instead of merging it.

   Angular's AOT compiler only emits declarables for components it owns as
   program inputs, so the SDK component sources are listed in `include` by their
   node_modules path (the path preserveSymlinks resolves them to); core needs no
   AOT but is listed so its sources join the same program. A dist consumer never
   needs any of this — the FESM ships everything pre-compiled. */
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.app.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": {
      "@angular/core": ["./node_modules/@angular/core"],
      "@angular/common": ["./node_modules/@angular/common"],
      "@hilos/angular": ["./node_modules/@hilos/angular/src/index.ts"],
      "@hilos/core": ["./node_modules/@hilos/core/src/index.ts"]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "src/**/*.ts",
    "node_modules/@hilos/angular/src/**/*.ts",
    "node_modules/@hilos/core/src/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["**/*.test.ts", "**/*.spec.ts", "src/prerender/**"]
}
